d/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1950.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1960.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1970.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1980.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1990.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2000.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2010.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2020.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2030.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2040.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2050.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2060.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2070.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2080.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2090.jpg'], 'response': 'Based on the provided set of 30 frames, here is a detailed description of the activities and elements present, analyzed according to your specified criteria:\n\n---\n\n**1) Presence of non-indigenous people (reporters/filmmakers) with cameras or recording equipment:**\n\n- **Direct Evidence:** While no camera or recording equipment is visible *in hand* in the live-action frames, **Frame 6, 7, and 8** show **photographs** of non-indigenous individuals, including one identified as **Bruno Manser** (a well-known Swiss activist who lived with the Penan). Frame 8 shows a smiling man (likely Manser or a similar figure) giving a thumbs-up in a jungle setting, suggesting he was documented during his time with the community.\n- **Indirect Evidence:** The **presence of subtitles** in Frames 3, 4, and 5 (“Is it working for every snake?”, “If any snake bites you, you will survive”, “Have you heard of Bruno Manser before?”) strongly implies an **interview or documentary-style interaction** between a filmmaker/narrator and the Penan man. This suggests a non-indigenous person is conducting the filming and asking questions.\n- **Frame 28 and 29** show close-up shots of a baby monkey being handled by **hands that appear to belong to a non-indigenous person** (lighter skin tone, different clothing style). This is consistent with a filmmaker or researcher documenting the interaction.\n- **Frame 20** shows young women taking selfies with smartphones, which may indicate the presence of modern technology and possibly outsiders, though not necessarily filmmakers.\n\n> **Conclusion:** There is strong indirect evidence of non-indigenous filmmakers or documentarians present, primarily through subtitles, photographic documentation, and the style of filming (close-ups, interviews). No direct shot of a camera is visible, but the context strongly implies their presence.\n\n---\n\n**2) Indigenous people teaching or demonstrating traditional skills (hunting, blowgun use, etc.):**\n\n- **Frame 1:** A Penan man is shown using a **machete** to cut or process something on a tree trunk — possibly preparing materials or demonstrating a forest skill.\n- **Frames 2–5:** A shirtless Penan man, adorned with traditional necklaces and arm bands, is **speaking directly to the camera**, with subtitles indicating he is explaining something about **snake bites and survival** — likely traditional medicinal knowledge using plants (as seen in Frame 3, he holds a leaf).\n- **Frame 9:** The same man is shown holding a **large knife or machete**, possibly demonstrating tool use or preparation for hunting or gathering.\n- **Frame 10 & 11:** Close-ups of large green leaves on a woven mat — possibly the same medicinal or ritual plant being referenced in the interview.\n- **Frame 24:** A drawing labeled “PANTERLUN” shows a hand with intricate traditional tattoo patterns — suggesting documentation or explanation of cultural body art.\n- **Frame 30:** A Penan man walks through a clearing near a small wooden hut, carrying what appears to be a **bag or bundle**, possibly returning from a foraging or hunting trip.\n\n> **Conclusion:** The Penan man is actively demonstrating and explaining traditional knowledge — specifically plant-based medicine for snake bites — and showcasing tools and cultural artifacts. This is clearly educational or documentary in nature.\n\n---\n\n**3) Scenes showing both jungle/forest environments and village/city environments:**\n\n- **Jungle/Forest Environments:**\n  - Frames 1–5, 7, 8, 9, 30: Dense green foliage, trees, undergrowth — classic tropical rainforest setting.\n  - Frame 13: A wide river surrounded by forested hills under a stormy sky — indicates riverine jungle environment.\n  - Frame 26: A grassy clearing with forested hills in the background and traditional stilt houses — transitional zone between jungle and settlement.\n\n- **Village/Settlement Environments:**\n  - Frame 14: A boat docked at a riverside settlement with people unloading goods — indicates a village or transport hub.\n  - Frame 15: A building with colorful traditional murals — likely a community center or longhouse.\n  - Frame 16: A skull and artifacts under a thatched roof — possibly a ritual or ancestral display in a village setting.\n  - Frame 17: A man on a motorcycle on a paved road — suggests access to modern infrastructure.\n  - Frame 18: Children playing volleyball in front of a two-story building — likely a school or community center.\n  - Frame 19: A woman serving rice wine to someone — social gathering in a village setting.\n  - Frame 21–23, 25: Indoor shots of a long hallway with people sitting, eating, and interacting — likely a school or communal building.\n  - Frame 26: Traditional wooden houses on stilts — typical Penan village architecture.\n  - Frame 27: A child sitting on a wooden platform with a baby monkey — domestic, village setting.\n\n> **Conclusion:** The video clearly juxtaposes **deep jungle scenes** with **village life**, including modern elements like roads, motorcycles, schools, and communal buildings. This highlights the coexistence of traditional and modern lifestyles.\n\n---\n\n**4) Any evidence of time duration or extended stay with the indigenous people:**\n\n- The **variety of settings and activities** — from jungle foraging (Frame 1), to interviews (Frames 2–5), to village life (Frames 14–15, 18–23), to cultural documentation (Frames 24, 26) — suggests the filming spanned **multiple days or even weeks**.\n- The **presence of photographs of Bruno Manser** (Frames 6–8) implies a historical context of long-term engagement with the Penan, and the current filming may be echoing or continuing that tradition.\n- The **intimate scenes** — such as the baby monkey being handled (Frames 27–29), the man with tattoos being filmed up close (Frames 21–23), and the casual social interactions (Frame 19, 21) — indicate a **level of trust and familiarity** that usually develops over time.\n- The **transition from jungle to village to school to road** suggests a **journey or immersion** rather than a brief visit.\n\n> **Conclusion:** The evidence strongly suggests an **extended stay** with the Penan community, allowing for deep cultural access and documentation across multiple contexts.\n\n---\n\n**5) Documentation activities like filming, photographing, or recording:**\n\n- As noted in point 1, **subtitles** indicate an interview is being conducted — a form of verbal documentation.\n- **Photographs** of Bruno Manser and others (Frames 6–8) are shown as artifacts — suggesting the film is documenting both past and present interactions.\n- **Close-up shots** (e.g., Frame 10–11, 28–29) and **intimate portraits** (Frame 21–23, 25) indicate deliberate, careful documentation of cultural details.\n- The **drawing of traditional tattoo patterns** (Frame 24) is itself a form of cultural documentation.\n- The **filming of daily life** — from children playing (Frame 18) to women serving rice wine (Frame 19) to men with tattoos (Frame 22) — shows a comprehensive ethnographic approach.\n\n> **Conclusion:** The video is clearly engaged in **active documentation** — through interviews, photography, close observation, and cultural artifact recording — consistent with ethnographic or documentary filmmaking.\n\n---\n\n**Final Summary:**\n\nThese 30 frames depict a **documentary-style immersion** with the Penan people of Borneo. A non-indigenous filmmaker (likely Raphael Treza, as per global summary) is present, conducting interviews and capturing traditional knowledge — particularly around medicinal plants and cultural practices. The Penan are shown actively demonstrating skills and sharing stories. The setting shifts between **dense jungle**, **riverside villages**, and **semi-modern communal spaces**, reflecting the community’s adaptation to change. The variety of scenes and the depth of interaction suggest an **extended stay**, allowing for rich, intimate documentation of both daily life and cultural heritage. The presence of historical photos and detailed cultural artifacts further reinforces the film’s ethnographic intent.'}, {'batch_start': 30, 'batch_end': 41, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2100.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2110.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2120.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2130.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2140.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2150.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2160.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2170.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2180.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2190.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2200.jpg'], 'response': "Based on the analysis of these 11 frames, here is a description of the activities and context:\n\n---\n\n**1) Presence of non-indigenous people (reporters/filmmakers) with cameras or recording equipment:**\n\n*   **No direct evidence** of non-indigenous people holding cameras or recording equipment is visible in these specific frames.\n*   However, **Frame 2** and **Frame 6** show a person (likely a woman or girl) wearing a **pink and black patterned shirt and shorts**, which appears to be **modern, non-traditional clothing**. This individual is observing or participating alongside the Penan people, suggesting they may be an outsider, potentially part of the film crew or a visitor being documented.\n*   The consistent, high-quality, and varied camera angles (low-angle shots, close-ups, wide shots) strongly imply the **presence of a professional or semi-professional filmmaker** operating the camera, even if they are not visible in these frames. The global summary also names Raphael Treza as a likely filmmaker.\n\n---\n\n**2) Indigenous people teaching or demonstrating traditional skills (hunting, blowgun use, etc.):**\n\n*   **Yes, this is clearly depicted.**\n*   **Frame 2 & 6:** A Penan man, wearing traditional adornments (headpiece, loincloth), is shown **demonstrating or using a blowgun**. In Frame 2, he is aiming it, and in Frame 6, he is standing on a simple wooden structure, possibly a platform or bridge, holding the blowgun.\n*   **Frame 3:** A close-up of a Penan man (likely the same one) actively **aiming a blowgun** at something off-screen, showcasing the technique.\n*   **Frame 5:** A Penan man is shown **crafting or preparing a tool** from bamboo or wood, using his hands and possibly a knife, demonstrating traditional craftsmanship.\n*   **Frame 9, 10, 11:** These frames show Penan individuals, including a young girl (Frame 11), **weaving or crafting with large palm leaves**. This demonstrates traditional skills in material culture and basketry/weaving. Frame 10 shows an adult guiding or working alongside the girl, suggesting **teaching or intergenerational knowledge transfer**.\n\n---\n\n**3) Scenes showing both jungle/forest environments and village/city environments:**\n\n*   **Jungle/Forest Environments:** **All 11 frames** are set within a **dense, lush tropical jungle or forest**. The background consistently features tall trees, thick undergrowth, ferns, and abundant greenery. There are no visible signs of urban or village infrastructure (houses, roads, etc.) in these specific frames.\n*   **Village/City Environments:** **None of these frames** depict village or city environments. The entire sequence shown here is focused exclusively on activities within the natural forest setting.\n\n---\n\n**4) Any evidence of time duration or extended stay with the indigenous people:**\n\n*   While a single sequence of 11 frames cannot definitively prove a long duration, the **variety of activities** shown (hunting with blowgun, crafting tools, weaving leaves, walking through the forest, interacting with children) suggests that the filming captured **multiple aspects of daily life or a specific day's activities**, rather than a single, brief moment.\n*   The **presence of children** participating or observing (Frame 6, 10, 11) and the **demonstration of skills** (implying a teaching context) can suggest a level of comfort and familiarity, which often comes with an **extended stay** allowing for deeper cultural immersion and documentation.\n*   The global summary also supports the idea of an ethnographic film, which typically involves prolonged fieldwork.\n\n---\n\n**5) Documentation activities like filming, photographing, or recording:**\n\n*   As mentioned in point 1, while **no recording equipment is visible in the hands of anyone in the frames**, the **cinematography itself is evidence of documentation**.\n*   The **diverse and deliberate camera angles** (low-angle shots in Frame 1 and 4, close-ups in Frame 3, 5, 9, 10, 11, wider shots in Frame 2, 6, 7, 8) indicate **intentional filming** to capture different perspectives and details of the activities.\n*   The **focus on specific actions** (aiming the blowgun, weaving leaves, crafting tools) suggests the filmmaker is **documenting traditional practices**.\n*   The presence of the individual in modern clothing (Frame 2, 6, 8) who is observing/participating could also be part of the documentation process, either as a subject being filmed or as an assistant.\n\n---\n\n**In Summary:**\n\nThese 11 frames depict a sequence of activities within the Bornean jungle, focusing on the Penan people demonstrating and practicing traditional skills such as blowgun hunting and leaf weaving. While no non-indigenous filmmaker is directly visible holding equipment, the presence of an individual in modern attire and the professional quality of the shots strongly imply active documentation. The entire sequence is set in the forest, with no village or city scenes shown. The variety of activities and interactions suggests the footage was captured over a period that allowed for observing multiple facets of daily life, consistent with an ethnographic documentary approach."}], 'formatted_responses': ['\n=== Batch 0-30 (30 frames) ===\nFrames: /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1800.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1810.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1820.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1830.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1840.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1850.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1860.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1870.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1880.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1890.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1900.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1910.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1920.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1930.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1940.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1950.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1960.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1970.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1980.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_1990.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2000.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2010.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2020.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2030.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2040.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2050.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2060.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2070.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2080.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2090.jpg\nVLM Response: Based on the provided set of 30 frames, here is a detailed description of the activities and elements present, analyzed according to your specified criteria:\n\n---\n\n**1) Presence of non-indigenous people (reporters/filmmakers) with cameras or recording equipment:**\n\n- **Direct Evidence:** While no camera or recording equipment is visible *in hand* in the live-action frames, **Frame 6, 7, and 8** show **photographs** of non-indigenous individuals, including one identified as **Bruno Manser** (a well-known Swiss activist who lived with the Penan). Frame 8 shows a smiling man (likely Manser or a similar figure) giving a thumbs-up in a jungle setting, suggesting he was documented during his time with the community.\n- **Indirect Evidence:** The **presence of subtitles** in Frames 3, 4, and 5 (“Is it working for every snake?”, “If any snake bites you, you will survive”, “Have you heard of Bruno Manser before?”) strongly implies an **interview or documentary-style interaction** between a filmmaker/narrator and the Penan man. This suggests a non-indigenous person is conducting the filming and asking questions.\n- **Frame 28 and 29** show close-up shots of a baby monkey being handled by **hands that appear to belong to a non-indigenous person** (lighter skin tone, different clothing style). This is consistent with a filmmaker or researcher documenting the interaction.\n- **Frame 20** shows young women taking selfies with smartphones, which may indicate the presence of modern technology and possibly outsiders, though not necessarily filmmakers.\n\n> **Conclusion:** There is strong indirect evidence of non-indigenous filmmakers or documentarians present, primarily through subtitles, photographic documentation, and the style of filming (close-ups, interviews). No direct shot of a camera is visible, but the context strongly implies their presence.\n\n---\n\n**2) Indigenous people teaching or demonstrating traditional skills (hunting, blowgun use, etc.):**\n\n- **Frame 1:** A Penan man is shown using a **machete** to cut or process something on a tree trunk — possibly preparing materials or demonstrating a forest skill.\n- **Frames 2–5:** A shirtless Penan man, adorned with traditional necklaces and arm bands, is **speaking directly to the camera**, with subtitles indicating he is explaining something about **snake bites and survival** — likely traditional medicinal knowledge using plants (as seen in Frame 3, he holds a leaf).\n- **Frame 9:** The same man is shown holding a **large knife or machete**, possibly demonstrating tool use or preparation for hunting or gathering.\n- **Frame 10 & 11:** Close-ups of large green leaves on a woven mat — possibly the same medicinal or ritual plant being referenced in the interview.\n- **Frame 24:** A drawing labeled “PANTERLUN” shows a hand with intricate traditional tattoo patterns — suggesting documentation or explanation of cultural body art.\n- **Frame 30:** A Penan man walks through a clearing near a small wooden hut, carrying what appears to be a **bag or bundle**, possibly returning from a foraging or hunting trip.\n\n> **Conclusion:** The Penan man is actively demonstrating and explaining traditional knowledge — specifically plant-based medicine for snake bites — and showcasing tools and cultural artifacts. This is clearly educational or documentary in nature.\n\n---\n\n**3) Scenes showing both jungle/forest environments and village/city environments:**\n\n- **Jungle/Forest Environments:**\n  - Frames 1–5, 7, 8, 9, 30: Dense green foliage, trees, undergrowth — classic tropical rainforest setting.\n  - Frame 13: A wide river surrounded by forested hills under a stormy sky — indicates riverine jungle environment.\n  - Frame 26: A grassy clearing with forested hills in the background and traditional stilt houses — transitional zone between jungle and settlement.\n\n- **Village/Settlement Environments:**\n  - Frame 14: A boat docked at a riverside settlement with people unloading goods — indicates a village or transport hub.\n  - Frame 15: A building with colorful traditional murals — likely a community center or longhouse.\n  - Frame 16: A skull and artifacts under a thatched roof — possibly a ritual or ancestral display in a village setting.\n  - Frame 17: A man on a motorcycle on a paved road — suggests access to modern infrastructure.\n  - Frame 18: Children playing volleyball in front of a two-story building — likely a school or community center.\n  - Frame 19: A woman serving rice wine to someone — social gathering in a village setting.\n  - Frame 21–23, 25: Indoor shots of a long hallway with people sitting, eating, and interacting — likely a school or communal building.\n  - Frame 26: Traditional wooden houses on stilts — typical Penan village architecture.\n  - Frame 27: A child sitting on a wooden platform with a baby monkey — domestic, village setting.\n\n> **Conclusion:** The video clearly juxtaposes **deep jungle scenes** with **village life**, including modern elements like roads, motorcycles, schools, and communal buildings. This highlights the coexistence of traditional and modern lifestyles.\n\n---\n\n**4) Any evidence of time duration or extended stay with the indigenous people:**\n\n- The **variety of settings and activities** — from jungle foraging (Frame 1), to interviews (Frames 2–5), to village life (Frames 14–15, 18–23), to cultural documentation (Frames 24, 26) — suggests the filming spanned **multiple days or even weeks**.\n- The **presence of photographs of Bruno Manser** (Frames 6–8) implies a historical context of long-term engagement with the Penan, and the current filming may be echoing or continuing that tradition.\n- The **intimate scenes** — such as the baby monkey being handled (Frames 27–29), the man with tattoos being filmed up close (Frames 21–23), and the casual social interactions (Frame 19, 21) — indicate a **level of trust and familiarity** that usually develops over time.\n- The **transition from jungle to village to school to road** suggests a **journey or immersion** rather than a brief visit.\n\n> **Conclusion:** The evidence strongly suggests an **extended stay** with the Penan community, allowing for deep cultural access and documentation across multiple contexts.\n\n---\n\n**5) Documentation activities like filming, photographing, or recording:**\n\n- As noted in point 1, **subtitles** indicate an interview is being conducted — a form of verbal documentation.\n- **Photographs** of Bruno Manser and others (Frames 6–8) are shown as artifacts — suggesting the film is documenting both past and present interactions.\n- **Close-up shots** (e.g., Frame 10–11, 28–29) and **intimate portraits** (Frame 21–23, 25) indicate deliberate, careful documentation of cultural details.\n- The **drawing of traditional tattoo patterns** (Frame 24) is itself a form of cultural documentation.\n- The **filming of daily life** — from children playing (Frame 18) to women serving rice wine (Frame 19) to men with tattoos (Frame 22) — shows a comprehensive ethnographic approach.\n\n> **Conclusion:** The video is clearly engaged in **active documentation** — through interviews, photography, close observation, and cultural artifact recording — consistent with ethnographic or documentary filmmaking.\n\n---\n\n**Final Summary:**\n\nThese 30 frames depict a **documentary-style immersion** with the Penan people of Borneo. A non-indigenous filmmaker (likely Raphael Treza, as per global summary) is present, conducting interviews and capturing traditional knowledge — particularly around medicinal plants and cultural practices. The Penan are shown actively demonstrating skills and sharing stories. The setting shifts between **dense jungle**, **riverside villages**, and **semi-modern communal spaces**, reflecting the community’s adaptation to change. The variety of scenes and the depth of interaction suggest an **extended stay**, allowing for rich, intimate documentation of both daily life and cultural heritage. The presence of historical photos and detailed cultural artifacts further reinforces the film’s ethnographic intent.\n', "\n=== Batch 30-41 (11 frames) ===\nFrames: /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2100.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/TiQBTesZUJQ/frames/frame_2110.jp