Frame 2, he is aiming it, and in Frame 6, he is standing on a simple wooden structure, possibly a platform or bridge, holding the blowgun.\n*   **Frame 3:** A close-up of a Penan man (likely the same one) actively **aiming a blowgun** at something off-screen, showcasing the technique.\n*   **Frame 5:** A Penan man is shown **crafting or preparing a tool** from bamboo or wood, using his hands and possibly a knife, demonstrating traditional craftsmanship.\n*   **Frame 9, 10, 11:** These frames show Penan individuals, including a young girl (Frame 11), **weaving or crafting with large palm leaves**. This demonstrates traditional skills in material culture and basketry/weaving. Frame 10 shows an adult guiding or working alongside the girl, suggesting **teaching or intergenerational knowledge transfer**.\n\n---\n\n**3) Scenes showing both jungle/forest environments and village/city environments:**\n\n*   **Jungle/Forest Environments:** **All 11 frames** are set within a **dense, lush tropical jungle or forest**. The background consistently features tall trees, thick undergrowth, ferns, and abundant greenery. There are no visible signs of urban or village infrastructure (houses, roads, etc.) in these specific frames.\n*   **Village/City Environments:** **None of these frames** depict village or city environments. The entire sequence shown here is focused exclusively on activities within the natural forest setting.\n\n---\n\n**4) Any evidence of time duration or extended stay with the indigenous people:**\n\n*   While a single sequence of 11 frames cannot definitively prove a long duration, the **variety of activities** shown (hunting with blowgun, crafting tools, weaving leaves, walking through the forest, interacting with children) suggests that the filming captured **multiple aspects of daily life or a specific day's activities**, rather than a single, brief moment.\n*   The **presence of children** participating or observing (Frame 6, 10, 11) and the **demonstration of